think The seam had token sheet, cascade, and Skate lowering all working in Rye, yet a designer could not yet AUTHOR a style from a .brush file — the vocabulary a design system targets was named but not read. This lap closes that: a .brush file declares its own style, and the parser reads it end to end.
think Delegated under the strict additive/stop-and-report/witness mandate (brush_parse is core with consumers). The grammar chosen is honest to the owned tongue: an optional `style:` header pin, sibling of `lines:`/`refuse:`, then two-space-indented `:: key value` flat-Bron token lines that reassemble verbatim into exactly the `key value` sheet tokens.rye's infuse already parses. No CSS/JS/JSON — the refuse-list holds; malformed or empty style blocks refuse with named errors.
think The full path now proves from a file on disk: styled-frame.brush declares Bark paper / Flax ink / warm edge / space 4, the parser collects it, tokens.resolve runs the infuse cascade, dress rasterizes 418 lit pixels in the declared colors, and a styled edge run seats. Source to pixels, witnessed.
obs Additive-default proven: a .brush with no style block parses byte-identically (seed-frame.brush unchanged — at-nib/present/max-lines/line-count identical, style-tokens=0). The in_style branch is only entered by a `style:` header, so no pre-style file touches new code. No parser contract or public type changed (fields appended); east_pack_test (which imports the parser) stays GREEN.
